            <ArticleTitle>Caring for a patient with a fungating malignant lesion in a hospice setting: reflecting on practice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>70-2, 74, 76-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This article attempts to demonstrate the process of using experiential learning to reflect on nursing practice. This will be discussed in relation to the Atkins and Murphy's model of reflection. A case study will be used to demonstrate the complex issues of symptom management and the implications for improving and maintaining a consistent quality of holistic care within a hospice. The case of 'Sylvia', a 68-year-old woman with a diagnosis of incurable cancer, will be discussed in relation to the management of a fungating malignant lesion, pain management, the control of malodour, and maintaining dignified and respectful care and optimizing self-esteem and quality of life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
